} = 30 \\).\n\n8. **Solve the equations**:\n   - Square both equations to eliminate the square roots:\n     \\[\n     (x - 33)^2 + y^2 = 1600\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     (x - 43)^2 + y^2 = 900\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the second equation from the first:\n     \\[\n     (x - 33)^2 - (x - 43)^2 = 700\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the difference of squares:\n     \\[\n     (x - 33 + x - 43)(x - 33 - (x - 43)) = 700\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     (2x - 76)(10) = 700\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     20x - 760 = 700\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     20x = 1460\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = 73\n     \\]\n   - Substitute \\( x = 73 \\) back into one of the equations to find \\( y \\):\n     \\[\n     (73 - 33)^2 + y^2 = 1600\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     40^2 + y^2 = 1600\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     1600 + y^2 = 1600\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     y^2 = 0\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     y = 0\n     \\]\n\n9. **Determine the area of \\( \\triangle BGE \\)**:\n   - The coordinates of \\( B \\) are \\( (43, 0) \\).\n   - The coordinates of \\( G \\) are \\( (73, 0) \\).\n   - The coordinates of \\( E \\) are \\( (64, 0) \\).\n\n10. **Use the formula for the area of a triangle**:\n    The area of \\( \\triangle BGE \\) can be calculated using the base \\( BE \\) and the height \\( y \\) (which is 0 in this case, but the height is actually the perpendicular distance from \\( G \\) to the line \\( BE \\), which is the y-coordinate of \\( G \\), which is 0).\n\n    The base \\( BE \\) is the distance between \\( B \\) and \\( E \\):\n    \\[\n    BE = 64 - 43 = 21\n    \\]\n\n    The height is the y-coordinate of \\( G \\), which is 0, but the perpendicular distance from \\( G \\) to the line \\( BE \\) is the y-coordinate of \\( G \\), which is 0.\n\n    The area of \\( \\triangle BGE \\) is:\n    \\[\n    \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 21 \\times 0 = 0\n    \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\( \\triangle BGE \\) is \\( \\boxed{0} \\).",
